# How this graph is written, read, checked and reviewed. The checker is tools/kg.c.

n: 8.1
name: node
kind: term
is: A node is one block of 'key: value' lines closed by a blank line. It holds one claim: one sentence or two in 'is', what kind of claim it is, how it is known, where it comes from, and the day it was written. Every node has this same shape at every depth, a hub included.
status: inferred
from: 4.3
at: 2026-11-24

n: 8.2
name: address
kind: term
is: The 'n' line is the node's address, a path of numbers such as 5.2.1. It is the stable handle other nodes cite. An address is never reused and never renumbered; a child is added by taking the next free number under its parent, so the graph grows at any depth without restructuring.
status: inferred
from: 4.3 1.3
why: An address is a name in the sense of axiom 3: a pointer that may move to new content. The numbers carry no meaning beyond position.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 8.3
name: digest
kind: term
is: A node's digest is the first 8 hex digits of SHA-256 over its lines without the 'n' line. It is the node's present content. It is computed by the checker on every run and stored nowhere except in a review, which names the digest it read.
status: inferred
from: 1.3 8.2
why: Address and digest are the two identities of axiom 3 kept apart: the name that moves and the blueprint that is. Storing the digest in the node would make every wording fix cascade through every citation.
flip: When the kernel can hold this graph as cells, the digest becomes the cell's own identity and this text becomes one lens over it.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 8.4
name: kinds
kind: term
is: Kinds are: hub (a part of the graph with a summary of its children), axiom and theorem (the given ground), term (a definition), source, observation (what a source shows), finding (what a study concluded from observations), decision, question, assumption, limit, plan, review.
status: assumed
at: 2026-11-24

n: 8.5
name: status
kind: term
is: Status says how a claim is known. given: the acknowledged ground, or guidance we hold together, kept in short form with 'by' naming where it came from. observed: someone in this project read the file at that line or ran the command. relayed: read through a tool that summarises, such as a web fetch, or through another report. inferred: reasoned from the nodes named in 'from'. assumed: not verified. A question is open or answered.
status: assumed
why: Web quotations reach a reader through a fetch tool that summarises; 'relayed' keeps that visible instead of calling it observed.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 8.6
name: sources-are-nodes
kind: decision
is: A source is a node under hub 2 with a 'path' or a 'url', the source's own 'date' where it has one, the revision read ('rev'), and the day it was read ('got'). A claim cites it as 'src: <address>#<locator>', where the locator is a line range or a section. A URL or path is written once, in its source node.
status: inferred
from: 4.3
at: 2026-11-24

n: 8.7
name: present-not-history
kind: decision
is: The graph holds what is known now. A node is edited in place and its 'at' date moves; git keeps every earlier state. The tree carries no superseded nodes, no record of rounds, no list of changes and no node that describes how another came to be. What no longer matches is taken out.
status: inferred
from: 1.3 2.1.3 8.14
alt: Immutable nodes with 'supersedes' links. Set aside because superseded copies would outweigh the living ones.
flip: If several writers work on separate branches for long stretches, immutable blocks merge more safely than edited ones.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 8.8
name: review
kind: term
is: A review is a node under hub 9 with 're' (the address reviewed), 'saw' (the digest it read), 'ack: 1' and 'by': an independent reader opened the claim's sources and confirms that wording. A reader who cannot confirm a claim changes the claim or raises a question; the graph keeps no record of the disagreement. A review whose 'saw' no longer equals the claim's digest is taken out by './kg release', and the claim is unreviewed again, which is not a verdict.
status: inferred
from: 1.5 1.7.19 8.3
at: 2026-11-24

n: 8.9
name: reduced-description
kind: decision
is: Each hub's 'is' line is a bounded summary of everything under it, so a part carries the shape of its whole at lower resolution. Reading node 0 and the hubs gives the entire graph in one screen; './kg brief' prints that view. This is what 'holographic' means here: a reduced description at every level, and the same node shape at every depth.
status: inferred
from: 4.3
limit: In the vector sense of the word, a text file with fields is the opposite of a holographic representation; the claim here is self-similarity with summaries, nothing stronger.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 8.10
name: files
kind: decision
is: A graph file is named by a node address and holds that node's descendants, the node itself included when it is not in its parent's file. File 0.kg holds the root and the top hubs. When a file passes 48 KB, a child hub moves to its own file; no address changes. Directory and file names carry no identity.
status: assumed
at: 2026-11-24

n: 8.11
name: english-is-one-lens
kind: limit
is: Addresses and digests are language-free, and every sentence in this graph is English. The graph is therefore one lens over claims whose language-neutral form does not exist yet. Names ('name' lines) are query keys and carry no authority.
status: inferred
from: 1.3 1.7.18
at: 2026-11-24

n: 8.12
name: format-is-unmeasured
kind: limit
is: The token cost of this block format under the tokenizers that will read it has not been measured, and neither has how reliably a local model writes it. Published comparisons of other formats and a byte count of one node are the only evidence behind the choice of key-value lines.
status: inferred
from: 4.3
at: 2026-11-24

n: 8.13
name: writing-a-node
kind: plan
is: To add a claim: pick its parent, take the next free number, write the block with its source or its premises, and run './kg check'. To change a claim: edit it in place, set 'at' to today, and run './kg release', which takes out any review that no longer matches the new wording. A claim without a source or premises is written as assumed, never left unmarked.
status: assumed
at: 2026-11-24

n: 8.14
name: only-what-is
kind: decision
is: The guide's direction of 2026-11-25: 'do not track history or corrections in documents, only keep what is, history shall only be in git, the git content shall always be like a green field, clean sheet without any trail on how we got here. we can keep a north star as direction, and we can keep a floor with observation date, and everything that is not aligned with what is now, shall be released and composted'. And: 'we just keep what is needed to close the gap to the north star from the now floor. everything else can be composted.'
status: given
by: guide
see: 8.7 7.10
at: 2026-11-25

n: 8.16
name: sources-resolve
kind: observation
is: The checker enforces that every source can be reached. A path starts with a repository's name; that repository is itself a source node carrying the web address and the revision it was read at; a path into this repository must be a file that exists. './kg link N' prints where the sources of node N can be read: a file here, or a web address pinned to a revision. Observed on 2026-11-25: a source file renamed away turned the check red.
status: observed
src: 2.30.1
limit: NUMS.Go has no published home: its node says 'url: unpublished', and its files were read from a checkout on the first machine at the revision named. A link pinned to a revision breaks if that repository's history changes.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 8.17
name: kept-while-referred
kind: decision
is: The ground, the design, what is open, the path and these rules (hubs 0, 1, 5, 6, 7 and 8) are living. A source, a piece of evidence or a review stays only while something living refers to it, directly or through what it refers to. './kg release' takes out everything else and removes a file left without nodes. Evidence is therefore exactly what the design needs to stand on, and it shrinks when a decision stops citing it.
status: inferred
from: 8.14 8.7
src: 2.30.1
conf: high
at: 2026-11-25

n: 8.15
name: checked-rules
kind: observation
is: The checker enforces: every node has an address, kind, statement and date; addresses are unique and each has its parent; every reference resolves; 'src' points at a source node; an observation has a source; an inferred claim has premises; a review names a digest and an acknowledgment; a node lives in a file named by one of its ancestors.
status: observed
src: 2.30.1
at: 2026-11-24

